  "response": "Reviewed the scan output and confirmed the asset on disk.\n\n## The finding\n\n`crime_00041` — **Oversized Raster**, `src/assets/hero-banner.png`\n\n> **byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)**\n> severity ladder: low ≥ 200 KB · medium ≥ 500 KB · high ≥ 1000 KB\n> format: png\n\nScores: `severity 0.4`, `confidence 0.95`, `agent_risk 0.14`, effort `quick`. It lands at the **bottom** of the 42 findings by aggregate risk — 41st of 42.\n\nOne thing the evidence doesn't give you: pixel dimensions. I had to read the file header for that — **1000 × 1000, 8-bit RGB, no alpha**. Worth knowing, because the size verdict and the resize recommendation both depend on it.\n\n## Verdict: request changes, don't block\n\nBlocking on this is disproportionate. It's a 253.9 KB image that clears the *low* threshold by 54 KB — the ladder puts a merge-blocking asset at ≥1000 KB, roughly 4× this. The confidence is 0.95 because it's a byte count, not an inference, so there's no false positive to argue about; but a high-confidence low-severity finding is still low severity.\n\nRequest changes rather than nit-and-merge for two reasons:\n\n1. **It's the LCP element.** A landing-page hero is the largest contentful paint by definition, so this 254 KB sits directly on the critical path for the page's Core Web Vitals. The same finding on a settings-page illustration would be a non-blocking nit.\n2. **The fix is `quick`.** Resize + re-encode is a five-minute change with `risk: low`. There's no reason to merge the debt and file a follow-up.\n\n**The bigger issue the detector can't see:** `hero-banner.png` is referenced nowhere in the repo — no import, no CSS `url()`, no markup. Either the PR is incomplete (asset landed without the component that renders it), or it's dead weight. Ask about that first; it changes whether the resize conversation matters at all.\n\n## The transformation\n\n**Dimensions — and a shape problem.** The source is square (1000 × 1000). A hero *banner* is a wide strip; a square source means either the browser is letterboxing it or CSS `object-fit: cover` is cropping away 60–70% of the pixels you're paying to download. Crop at build time to the actual aspect ratio — something like **1000 × 420** (≈21:9) or **1000 × 560** (16:9) — and you cut the payload roughly in half before touching the encoder.\n\nThe harder constraint: at 1000 px wide, this source **cannot** serve a full-bleed hero. On a 1440 px viewport at DPR 2 you'd need 2880 px of image; you have 1000. If the hero is full-bleed, the correct fix is to get a larger original from design, not to re-encode this one. If the render area is a constrained column (say 500 px CSS), 1000 px is exactly right for DPR 2 and you only need the format change.\n\nShip it responsive either way:\n\n```html\n<picture>\n  <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"hero-640.avif 640w, hero-960.avif 960w, hero-1280.avif 1280w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"hero-640.webp 640w, hero-960.webp 960w, hero-1280.webp 1280w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <img src=\"hero-960.webp\" width=\"1280\" height=\"538\" alt=\"…\" fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\">\n</picture>\n```\n\nExplicit `width`/`height` to avoid CLS, `fetchpriority=\"high\"` because it's the LCP image, and **no** `loading=\"lazy\"` — lazy-loading the LCP element makes the metric worse.\n\n**AVIF, with a WebP fallback.** For this specific asset:\n\n- **The content class favours AVIF.** This is photographic RGB with no alpha — exactly where AVIF's advantage is largest, typically **~30% smaller than WebP** at matched perceptual quality. Expect roughly 35–60 KB at AVIF q50–63 for 1000 px, versus 80–110 KB for WebP q80. Against 254 KB that's an 80%+ reduction.\n- **No transparency to preserve**, so lossy encoding is free — nothing forces you to keep PNG.\n- **Support is no longer the tiebreaker.** AVIF has been available across all major engines since Edge shipped it in early 2024; WebP is universal. The `<picture>` fallback covers the tail regardless, and costs one extra `<source>` line.\n\nTwo cases where I'd pick WebP instead: if the hero has **text or hard-edged graphics baked into the raster** (AVIF's low-bitrate smoothing and chroma handling degrade crisp edges more visibly than WebP does), or if you're doing **on-the-fly transforms at request time** — AVIF encode is markedly slower than WebP, which matters for an image CDN's cold-cache path but not for a build-time asset like this one.\n\nGenerate both at build time; the encoder cost is paid once.",
